---
import Base from '../../../layouts/Base.astro';

const example = `match local.clock.compare(&remote.clock) {
    Order::Before => MergeDecision::UseRemote,
    Order::After => MergeDecision::KeepLocal,
    Order::Concurrent => MergeDecision::Conflict,
    Order::Equal if local.value == remote.value => MergeDecision::Same,
    Order::Equal => MergeDecision::Conflict,
}`;
---
<Base lang="en" title="Local sync Rust sample | Miguel" description="Independent Rust example with vector clocks, conflict detection and an acknowledged outbox.">
  <main id="contenido" class="detail-main wrap">
    <a class="back-link" href="/en/lab/">← Back to the lab</a>
    <div class="detail-hero">
      <h1 class="long-title">Local changes</h1>
      <p class="detail-lede">A small Rust sample separating causal ordering from concurrent writes and retrying unacknowledged operations.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="detail-meta">
      <div><span>Causality</span><strong>Vector clocks</strong></div>
      <div><span>Delivery</span><strong>Acknowledged outbox</strong></div>
      <div><span>Verification</span><strong>5 local tests</strong></div>
    </div>
    <div class="case-layout">
      <aside><span>Problem</span><span>Decision</span><span>Code</span><span>Limits</span></aside>
      <div class="case-content">
        <section><h2>Two devices can edit the same record.</h2><p>When both work offline, a timestamp cannot show whether one change had observed the other. The sample uses per actor counters to identify causal order and concurrency.</p></section>
        <section><h2>Detect before resolving.</h2><p>A causally newer clock can win. Concurrent clocks produce an explicit conflict for product rules to resolve. Outgoing operations remain pending until acknowledged.</p></section>
        <section><h2>The comparison is explicit.</h2><pre class="sample-code"><code>{example}</code></pre><p>The full code and tests are in <code>samples/local-sync-rust</code> inside this independent portfolio repository.</p></section>
        <section><h2>A sample, not a production engine.</h2><p>It has no durable persistence, transport, authentication, encryption or automatic conflict policy. It contains no private Meson code.</p></section>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="detail-actions"><a class="text-link" style="margin-top:0" href="/en/projects/red-p2p/">Read the Meson P2P case ↗</a></div>
  </main>
</Base>
